<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen</h1><p class="st-meta">1599 CE – 1920 CE · capital Sondershausen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Thuringian principality of the house of Schwarzburg, ruled from Sondershausen and Arnstadt, where the young Bach held his first organ post.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stadtilm">Treaty of Stadtilm</a> of 1599 split the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwarzburg">Schwarzburg</a> lands, and Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-gunther-i-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen">John Günther I</a>'s line received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>, forming Schwarzburg-Sondershausen alongside the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-schwarzburg-rudolstadt">Principality of Schwarzburg-Rudolstadt</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts became princes in 1697 and ruled a Lutheran territory in two parts, the Lower Lordship around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sondershausen">Sondershausen</a> and the Upper Lordship around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnstadt">Arnstadt</a>. Arnstadt gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> his first post as organist in 1703. The princes kept a court orchestra, and in the 19th century Sondershausen became known for its public concerts and music school.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The principality joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871. When Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-gunther-prince-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen">Karl Günther</a> died childless in 1909, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunther-victor-prince-of-schwarzburg">Günther Victor of Rudolstadt</a> inherited it in personal union. He abdicated in November 1918, and in 1920 the state merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-state-of-thuringia">Free State of Thuringia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen.jpg" alt="Principality of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: Kamradden · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Schwarzburg_Sondershausen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1599 CE – 1920 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sondershausen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian William, Karl Günther</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Schwarzburg-Sondershausen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1760&amp;focus=principality-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen" aria-label="Principality of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4926 399 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Schwarzburg-Sondershausen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5246 655L5249 670M5246 655L5229 694M5246 655L5300 622M5246 655L5250 665"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5249 670h0M5229 694h0M5300 622h0M5250 665h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5246 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5246 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sondershausen. <a href="/atlas?year=1760&amp;focus=principality-of-schwarzburg-sondershausen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
